me":true,"pf":14.0,"pa":13.0,"off_snaps":62,"epa_per_play":-0.01365,"pass_rate":0.4355,"pass_rate_neutral":0.4643,"def_epa_per_play":0.05051},{"week":16,"opponent":"NE","home":false,"pf":12.0,"pa":24.0,"off_snaps":60,"epa_per_play":-0.3456,"pass_rate":0.7,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5294,"def_epa_per_play":-0.1892},{"week":17,"opponent":"MIA","home":true,"pf":42.0,"pa":17.0,"off_snaps":58,"epa_per_play":0.2551,"pass_rate":0.4655,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5,"def_epa_per_play":-0.3758}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2024,"team":"GB","distance":0.4785,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Matt LaFleur"},{"season":2021,"team":"DET","distance":0.5861,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Anthony Lynn"},{"season":2019,"team":"WAS","distance":0.6036,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"ARI","distance":0.6053,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"NYJ","distance":0.6064,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Tanner Engstrand"}],"defense":[{"season":2020,"team":"WAS","distance":0.5539,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"MIN","distance":0.5974,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"JAX","distance":0.6054,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2021,"team":"CLE","distance":0.621,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"SF","distance":0.6317,"shared":18,"play_caller":"DeMeco Ryans"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Bills went 6-10 in 2018 and were outscored 374 to 269. The defense did most of the work, finishing 3rd in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 31st in EPA per play. Their most distinctive traits were the 2nd lowest early-down pass rate and the highest Cover 4 rate in the league.","offense":"The Bills offense struggled, ranking 25th or worse in all five main efficiency measures. They were one of the most run-heavy teams in the league, passing less often than game situations would predict. Josh Allen ran the passing game, with Zay Jones leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Bills had one of the best defenses in the league, ranking 7th or better in three of the five main efficiency measures. Its strongest area was EPA allowed per dropback, where it ranked 2nd, and its weakest was explosive play rate allowed, where it ranked 18th. Jerry Hughes led the team with 7 sacks, and Trent Murphy added 4 despite playing only 53% of the snaps. In terms of scheme, they had the highest Cover 4 rate in the league.","special":"The Bills were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 81% of field goals, which was a little below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into running backs, about 11% of the money on the books. Their running backs were among the oldest in the league.","players":"Josh Allen played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 2,044 yards. Targets were spread around, with Zay Jones and Kelvin Benjamin leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. The backfield was a committee led by LeSean McCoy and Chris Ivory. On defense, Jerry Hughes led the team with 7 sacks and Jordan Poyer picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"Brian Daboll called the offense, and the defensive play caller for this season is not in the coaching data. Their offense looked most like the 2024 Packers under Matt LaFleur and the 2021 Lions under Anthony Lynn. Their defense looked most like the 2020 Commanders and the 2018 Vikings.","trends":"2018 is the first season covered here, so there is no earlier year to compare it with. The charts below show how the Bills changed over the seasons that followed.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2018. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}